              "text": "**Yes, Coca-Cola did originally contain cocaine.** \n\nThe history of how the famous soft drink got its name\u2014and how it eventually removed the drug while keeping the leaf\u2014is a fascinating tale of 19th-century medicine and 20th-century chemistry.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Origin Story\nCoca-Cola was invented in 1885 by Dr. John Stith Pemberton, an Atlanta pharmacist and former Confederate soldier who had developed a morphine addiction after being wounded in the Civil War. Seeking a cure for his addiction, he began experimenting with coca leaves. \n\nPemberton\u2019s creation, originally a coca-wine called \"Pemberton's French Wine Coca,\" was modified into a non-alcoholic syrup after Atlanta passed local temperance laws in 1885. He named this new beverage **Coca-Cola**, directly referencing its two key ingredients:\n*   **\"Coca\"** from the South American coca leaf (which naturally contains cocaine).\n*   **\"Cola\"** from the African kola nut (a natural source of caffeine).\n\n### 2. How Much Cocaine Was in It?\nDuring the late 19th century, Coca-Cola was marketed as a \"brain tonic\" and patent medicine. Historians estimate that early glasses of Coca-Cola contained about **9 milligrams of cocaine** per serving. \n\nTo put this in perspective, a recreational \"line\" of powdered cocaine today typically contains about 50 to 75 milligrams. So while a glass of original Coca-Cola wouldn't produce a massive high, it was certainly enough to give drinkers a noticeable, euphoric \"buzz\". \n\n### 3. Why and When Was It Removed?\nBy the turn of the 20th century, public and medical perceptions of cocaine shifted dramatically. Initially praised as a miracle cure for depression and exhaustion, it was increasingly recognized as a highly addictive and destructive drug. \n\nThis shift was heavily accelerated by racial and social anxieties in the American South. White-dominated newspapers and politicians stoked fears that cocaine was giving Black laborers superhuman strength and driving them to crime. \n\nTo protect the company's image and avoid looming government regulations, Coca-Cola's leadership (then under Asa Candler) decided to remove the drug:\n*   **In 1903**, the company transitioned from using fresh coca leaves to using \"spent\" (decocainized) coca leaves. \n*   Because the decocainizing technology of the early 1900s wasn't perfect, microscopic trace amounts of cocaine remained in the drink for some years.\n*   **By 1929**, the extraction process was fully perfected, and Coca-Cola became **100% cocaine-free**.\n\n### 4. The Modern Secret: Coca-Cola Still Imports Coca Leaves\nThough there is absolutely no cocaine in Coca-Cola today, **the company still uses the coca leaf as an ingredient.**\n\nTo maintain its signature, secret flavor profile, Coca-Cola relies on a special arrangement with the U.S. government. A chemical processing facility in Maywood, New Jersey, operated by the **Stepan Company**, holds the exclusive license from the Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) to legally import and process coca leaves from South America. \n\nAt the plant, Stepan Company extracts the active cocaine alkaloid from the leaves. The \"spent,\" decocainized leaf extract is sent directly to Coca-Cola to flavor the soda. The extracted cocaine is not thrown away; it is sold to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which uses it to manufacture legal, prescription local anesthetics used by doctors for eye, nose, and throat surgeries.",
